#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM5020", unit: "U2")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U2.1 Cosets",[List the cosets of $H=\{[0],[4],[8]\}$ in $ZZ slash 12 ZZ$.],[Translate $H$ by small residues.],[There are $12/3=4$ cosets.],[They are $\{0,4,8\}$, $\{1,5,9\}$, $\{2,6,10\}$, and $\{3,7,11\}$, with entries understood modulo twelve. They are disjoint and cover all twelve residues.],[List each coset once and account for all elements.]),
 p(2,"E","U2.1 Lagrange",[What element orders are possible in a group of order twenty-one according to Lagrange's theorem? Does this alone guarantee an element of order twenty-one?],[List the positive divisors.],[A necessary condition need not be sufficient.],[The possible orders permitted by Lagrange are $1,3,7,21$. Lagrange gives no existence conclusion for order twenty-one; it only says that an element's order, if it occurs, must divide twenty-one.],[Distinguish an allowed order from a guaranteed order.]),
 p(3,"E","U2.2 Permutation composition",[Compute $(123)(23)$ and its sign, composing right to left.],[Track the images of one, two, and three.],[One maps to two, two maps to one, and three is fixed.],[The product is $(12)$. Its sign is negative. Independently, the three-cycle is even and the transposition is odd, so the product is odd.],[State the composition convention and verify images.]),
 p(4,"E","U2.2 Cycle order",[Find the order and sign of $(1234)(567)$ in $S_7$.],[The cycles are disjoint.],[Take the least common multiple and multiply their signs.],[The order is $"lcm"(4,3)=12$. The four-cycle has sign $(-1)^3=-1$ and the three-cycle sign $(-1)^2=1$, so the product has sign $-1$.],[Do not add cycle lengths when computing order.]),
 p(5,"C","U2.1 Coset criteria",[Prove $a H=b H$ if and only if $a^(-1)b in H$.],[Start with membership of $b$ in $a H$.],[Write $b=a h$.],[If the cosets agree, $b in a H$, so $b=a h$ and $a^(-1)b=h in H$. Conversely, if $b=a h$ with $h in H$, then $b H=a h H=a H$ because multiplication by $h$ permutes $H$.],[Give both implications; either inverse orientation is valid if used consistently.]),
 p(6,"C","U2.1 Prime order",[Prove that a group of prime order has no proper nontrivial subgroup and is cyclic.],[Apply Lagrange twice.],[The subgroup generated by a nonidentity element has order greater than one.],[Any subgroup has order dividing the prime $p$, hence order one or $p$. For $g!=e$, $chevron.l g chevron.r$ is not the trivial subgroup, so it has order $p$ and equals the whole group.],[Explain the existence of a nonidentity element and why its generated subgroup is nontrivial.]),
 p(7,"C","U2.2 Powers of cycles",[Describe the disjoint cycles of $(123456)^2$ and $(123456)^3$, and compute their orders.],[Adding the exponent modulo six describes the action on positions.],[The cycles are the orbits of adding two or three.],[The square is $(135)(246)$ and has order three. The cube is $(14)(25)(36)$ and has order two. These agree with $6/gcd(6,2)$ and $6/gcd(6,3)$.],[Show the actual cycles, not only the order formula.]),
 p(8,"C","U2.2 Counting permutations",[How many three-cycles are in $S_5$? How many products of two disjoint transpositions?],[Choose supports before choosing arrangements.],[A three-element support has two cycles; a four-element support has three pairings.],[There are $binom(5,3) dot 2=20$ three-cycles. For double transpositions choose the omitted fixed point in five ways, then one of three pairings of the remaining four points, giving fifteen.],[Avoid counting rotations of a cycle or orders of disjoint factors as different permutations.]),
 p(9,"C","U2.2 Alternating groups",[Show every three-cycle is even and every even permutation is a product of three-cycles.],[Pair the transpositions in an even factorization.],[A pair sharing a letter is a three-cycle; a disjoint pair can be split using an intermediate transposition.],[A three-cycle is a product of two transpositions, so is even. Pair transpositions in an even factorization. Equal pairs cancel; pairs sharing one letter form a three-cycle or its inverse. For disjoint transpositions, $(a b)(c d)=[(a b)(b c)][(b c)(c d)]$, since the middle transpositions cancel. Each bracket is a three-cycle. Thus every pair can be replaced by three-cycles.],[Handle equal, overlapping, and disjoint pairs.]),
 p(10,"A","U2.2 Minimal permutation degree",[Find the least $n$ for which $S_n$ has an element of order fifteen.],[Cycle lengths must have least common multiple fifteen.],[Compare one fifteen-cycle with disjoint cycles of lengths three and five.],[Disjoint cycles of lengths three and five give order fifteen on eight letters. If fewer than eight letters are used, no cycle can have length fifteen. To make the least common multiple divisible by both three and five, there must therefore be distinct cycles with lengths divisible by three and five; these use at least eight letters. Hence the least degree is eight.],[Prove the lower bound, not merely exhibit an example.]),
 p(11,"A","U2.1 Indices in a tower",[For finite $G$ and $K<=H<=G$, prove $[G:K]=[G:H][H:K]$ by constructing coset representatives.],[Choose representatives for both stages.],[Products of representatives give representatives for $G slash K$.],[Let $g_i$ represent $G slash H$ and $h_j$ represent $H slash K$. Every $g$ is $g_i h$ and then $g_i h_j k$, so the products represent all cosets modulo $K$. If $g_i h_j K=g_(i')h_(j')K$, then the elements lie in the same coset modulo $H$, forcing $i=i'$. Cancelling $g_i$ forces $h_j K=h_(j')K$ and hence $j=j'$. Count the products.],[Establish both coverage and uniqueness.]),
 p(12,"A","U2.2 Conjugation and cycles",[Prove $tau(a_1 dots a_r)tau^(-1)=(tau(a_1) dots tau(a_r))$. Deduce that conjugate permutations have the same cycle lengths, and conversely.],[Evaluate the conjugate on $tau(a_i)$.],[For the converse, choose a bijection matching corresponding cycle positions.],[The conjugate sends $tau(a_i)$ to $tau(a_(i+1))$ cyclically and fixes points outside the displayed support. Thus conjugation relabels each cycle and preserves lengths. Conversely, if two permutations have matching cycle lengths, define $tau$ by matching the ordered entries of corresponding cycles, including fixed points. The displayed formula makes the conjugate equal to the target permutation.],[Include fixed points and verify that the matching defines a bijection.]),
)
